sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-pa01-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The division of the backbone into vertebrae is not, as Empedocles held, the result of the backbone being broken by the contorted position of the foetus in the womb, nor are animal characters in general mere incidents of development.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The vertebrae are not formed by accidental breaking of the foetal backbone";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, On the Parts of Animals (De Partibus Animalium), Book I, Part 1; translated by William Ogle, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. V (1912). Scope: animals with a backbone (vertebrates)"
}
